# Official v1.38 Rust frontend experiment

This experiment recompiles the same 812-file recovered Rust source map as candidate11, with the same canonical image, release profile and lockfile, selecting the separately downloaded official Linux platform-tools v1.38 rather than v1.37. No captured executable, assembly or instruction table is supplied to the compiler. No byte identity is accepted unless the actual output matches the full capture.

Both SDKs report LLVM15.0.7 and share the LLVM source revision. Their Rust source revisions differ: v1.37 records `764ce69bd00903f5c59f44b6aa72649b173f568c`, v1.38 records `768cf399311f6e6a716652a1a4397d35495880d7`. The published Rust delta includes SBF default-hidden visibility and OOM code changes; this makes a controlled frontend-family test useful despite the common LLVM revision. It does not imply which version produced the capture.

`sdk-files.json` retains SHA256 values for all 1989 extracted files in each distribution. Forty-four paths differ. The 9152-byte rustc launcher scripts are identical, SHA256 `7194df9c12e893cfa88fff5dd821d02d45910b4d375e13677b4723f45769a384`; the actual `librustc_driver-f767167903553d17.so` hashes differ (`e7d22150…` versus `ae7f225f…`). Launcher identity alone cannot establish compiler closure identity. `archive-provenance.json` records download URLs and local archive hashes; these are integrity records, not historical publisher attestations.

`build.py` adapts the validated local Linux driver by explicitly requesting `--tools-version v1.38` and mounting that distribution at the versioned SDK cache path. A separate fresh source checkout, target directory and container avoid contaminating the validated v1.37 baseline. The public registry cache is shared. The readonly mount, selected toolchain symlink and completed compiler selection must be retained with actual output before judging this trial. The user requires reconstructed Rust builds.

The invoked driver is frozen beside baseline output. The reusable driver adds a full SDK file-map assertion after that invocation; the measured SDK closure was also independently hashed during the run. This additional preflight is disclosed rather than retroactively attributed to the invoked driver.

## Measured result

The Rust compilation finished successfully in 6m29s using the selected v1.38 toolchain. The SDK's subsequent `strip.sh` sources `env.sh`, installs/selects its default v1.37 SDK and runs that distribution's llvm-objcopy. The actual scripts, before-strip compiler output, in-flight v1.38 symlink observation and post-build v1.37 tool report are retained. The inherited post-build `tool-versions.json` describes the **post-strip** selection and must not be mislabeled as the compiler phase. The original cargo-build-sbf source explains the separate strip-script invocation. Compiler and strip phases were kept distinct; no manual reference-derived transformation was used.

The stripped output is raw-identical to candidate11: 707448 bytes, SHA256 `3bbaf27ff0c10ff68d8a195a82f39eadee266f324067bd0a66081710be30b4ec`. Its complete executable section equals candidate11 and differs from the capture. Full allocated payload comparison differs at1509 positions. No source or byte identity is newly accepted. This excludes this measured compiler/source combination, not every v1.38 source form or unknown historical pipeline.

A second ordinary invocation of the selected v1.37 llvm-objcopy on the retained compiler-generated unstripped ELF reproduces the same stripped output. Its executable hash matches the complete v1.37 SDK map. This is a stripping control on actual Rust compiler output, not patching captured executable bytes.

The reusable driver now additionally requires `--strip-sdk` and binds the verified v1.37 distribution read-only, alongside v1.38. That avoids the default postcompile download while preserving the measured stripping phase. This preflight and mount were added **after** the recorded invocation; `baseline/invoked-driver.py` preserves what actually ran. Raw download logs remain in work; the published log removes temporary query strings from public release-download URLs and binds both hashes in `log-redaction.json`.

Run `python3 repro/pump/metaplex-v138-research/verify.py` for offline evidence validation. For another fresh source build, invoke build.py with `--dependency token_metadata --metaplex-zero-use-recovery`, a clean public checkout at the pinned commit, a fresh output directory, `--sdk` pointing to official Linux v1.38, `--strip-sdk` to official Linux v1.37, and `--registry-cache` to a public crate cache. The shared source-only helper remains unchanged.
